WEBVTT

NOTE
English subtitles of "Solar System explained by simpleshow foundation", from Wikimedia Commons
(TimedText:Solar System explained by simpleshow foundation.webm.en.srt) by Konarkm68 and Kai Burghardt,
CC BY-SA 4.0. Checked against the audio; four wordings corrected (cues 23, 28, 42, 44).

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
We explain:
The Solar System.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:04.500
This is the Sun,

3
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.000
the star of our Solar System.

4
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.500
Every object in its vicinity
is attracted towards it.

5
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.500
It is the biggest energy source and
the gravitational center in our Solar System.

6
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.500
To understand gravity
let’s go back some billions of years ago.

7
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:26.250
During this time, the space was not filled with stars
but with a gigantic gas cloud.

8
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.000
The elements in this cloud
were drawn to each other.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.500
The more massive objects become
the stronger they attract other objects.

10
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.000
This effect is called gravitation.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Most of the matter
accumulated in one place:

12
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.750
A very big hot ball

13
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.500
which makes up for an impressive 98 %
of all the matter in the Solar System:

14
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:48.750
The Sun.

15
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.500
From the center of our Solar System

16
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.500
it exerts gravitational force
at all objects around it.

17
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.500
Besides gravity, other forces
can influence an object in space.

18
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.500
Together, these forces define
the movement of all objects.

19
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.500
If a celestial body revolves around another
we call this movement an orbit,

20
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.750
like our Moon,
which orbits the Earth.

21
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.500
8 planets orbit the Sun.

22
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The inner ones are
Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars.

23
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.750
And the outer planets are
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune.

24
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:33.750
The Asteroid Belt and the so‑called
trans-Neptune objects like the dwarf planet Pluto

25
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.000
are also revolving around the Sun.

26
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.000
But the Sun is not only the
gravitational center of our Solar System.

27
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.000
It is also a source of energy:

28
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:49.500
Fueled by nuclear fission, the Sun emits light
and a steady stream of particles, the solar wind.

29
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.750
Sometimes you can see it from Earth.

30
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.250
This spectacle is called aurora borealis,
or the northern lights,

31
00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.500
mostly seen in the vicinity
of the poles of our planet.

32
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.250
The distance between the Earth and
the Sun is called an astronomical unit.

33
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.750
This entity is used to
measure distances in space.

34
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.000
The last planet of the
Solar System, Neptune,

35
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.750
is 30 AU away from the Sun.

36
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.500
The solar wind travels
up to 150 AU.

37
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.250
Its reach is called
the heliosphere.

38
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.500
But the Solar System is much bigger.

39
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.500
Its boundaries reach as far as
the influence of the Sun’s gravity.

40
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.000
It extends to a place
called the Oort cloud,

41
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
where most of the comets
are believed to come from.

42
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.250
Up to 100,000 astronomical units away.

43
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.500
The places at the edge of our
Solar System are largely unexplored,

44
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.750
and we are still working to uncover
the secrets hidden in the depths of space.

45
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:55.000
But our Sun would not be a real star without
being surrounded by an air of mystery, would it?
